<CalculatorLayout
  title="Heart Rate Zones Calculator"
  description="Calculate your target heart rate zones free. Optimize your workouts using the Karvonen method with resting heart rate. 100% private."
  keyword="heart rate calculator"
  canonicalPath="/calculator/heart-rate"
  formula="Karvonen Method (incorporating RHR)"
  lastModified={new Date('2026-07-02')}
  faqs={faqs}
  relatedCalculators={relatedCalculators}
  accuracyRating="±5–10%"
>

  <!-- ── SLOT: calculator ── -->
  <HeartRateCalculator slot="calculator" />

  <!-- ── SLOT: what-is ── -->
  <Fragment slot="what-is">
    <p>
      A <strong>heart rate zones calculator</strong> is an essential tool for cardiorespiratory fitness
      and endurance training. Your heart rate, measured in beats per minute (bpm), is a direct reflection of
      how hard your cardiovascular system is working during physical exertion. By establishing your training
      zones, you can ensure that your workouts target specific physiological adaptations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Standard training protocols divide heart rate intensity into five zones. <strong>Zone 1</strong> is
      for light warm-ups and active recovery. <strong>Zone 2</strong> focuses on building your aerobic base
      and maximizing fat oxidation. <strong>Zone 3</strong> targets moderate cardio endurance.
      <strong>Zone 4</strong> pushes your lactate threshold (the point where lactic acid begins to accumulate
      faster than it can be cleared), and <strong>Zone 5</strong> challenges your anaerobic peak and VO₂ max.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our calculator utilizes the <strong>Karvonen Method</strong>. While most basic calculators simply take
      percentages of your estimated maximum heart rate (Max HR = 220 − age), the Karvonen method calculates
      zones using your <strong>Heart Rate Reserve (HRR)</strong>. Your HRR is the difference between your max
      heart rate and your resting heart rate. By factoring in your resting heart rate, the Karvonen method
      accounts for your current level of physical fitness, making the resulting zones far more accurate.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, two 40-year-olds will have the same estimated Max HR (180 bpm). However, if one has a resting
      heart rate of 50 bpm (very fit) and the other has 80 bpm (sedentary), their cardiovascular systems will
      operate differently. The Karvonen method adjusts the zones for both individuals to match their biological
      realities.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ── SLOT: how-to ── -->
  <Fragment slot="how-to">
    <p>
      The Karvonen formula calculates target heart rate (THR) by multiplying your Heart Rate Reserve by the
      desired intensity percentage, then adding back your resting heart rate.
    </p>

    <div class="formula-block" role="math" aria-label="Karvonen heart rate formulas">
      <p class="formula-label">The Karvonen Equations</p>
      <code class="formula-code">1. Calculate Maximum Heart Rate (MHR)
   Max HR = 220 − Age

2. Determine Heart Rate Reserve (HRR)
   HRR = Max HR − Resting Heart Rate

3. Calculate Target Heart Rate (THR) for a Zone
   THR = (HRR × Intensity %) + Resting Heart Rate

Example: 30 years old, 60 bpm resting HR, Zone 2 lower bound (60%):
   Max HR = 220 − 30 = 190 bpm
   HRR = 190 − 60 = 130 bpm
   THR = (130 × 0.60) + 60
       = 78 + 60 = 138 bpm</code>
      <p class="formula-source">
        Source: Karvonen M, Kentala E, Mustala O. (1957). "The effects of training on heart rate: a longitudinal
        study." <em>Annales Medicinae Experimentalis et Biologiae Fenniae</em>, 35(3), 307–315.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Training Application</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find your RHR by taking your pulse over 60 seconds first thing in the morning.</li>
      <li>Enter your age and RHR into the calculator to generate your custom ranges.</li>
      <li>Wear a heart rate monitor during exercise and aim to keep your beats per minute within your chosen training zone.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ── SLOT: ranges ── -->
  <Fragment slot="ranges">
    <p>
      Understanding the physiological benefits of each heart rate zone allows you to structure your
      cardio sessions for maximum efficiency.
    </p>

    <h3>Heart Rate Zones Adaptations</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Zone</th>
          <th>Intensity (of HRR)</th>
          <th>Energy Source Used</th>
          <th>Training Benefits</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Zone 1</strong> (Warm Up)</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">50 – 60%</td>
          <td>Mostly fat</td>
          <td>Active recovery, boosts blood circulation, decreases muscle soreness.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Zone 2</strong> (Aerobic Base)</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">60 – 70%</td>
          <td>Fat (highest ratio)</td>
          <td>Builds mitochondrial density, improves aerobic capacity and endurance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Zone 3</strong> (Aerobic Tempo)</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">70 – 80%</td>
          <td>Mixed (fat/carbs)</td>
          <td>Improves cardiovascular efficiency, raises standard pacing threshold.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Zone 4</strong> (Anaerobic)</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">80 – 90%</td>
          <td>Mostly carbohydrates</td>
          <td>Increases lactate threshold, improves high-intensity stamina.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Zone 5</strong> (Peak VO₂ Max)</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">90 – 100%</td>
          <td>Carbohydrates</td>
          <td>Improves maximal oxygen uptake (VO₂ max), sprint power, and speed.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Structuring Your Cardio</h3>
    <p>
      For optimal fitness and metabolic health, most exercise scientists recommend the <strong>80/20 rule
      (polarized training)</strong>. This means spending approximately 80% of your weekly training volume
      in low-intensity zones (Zone 2) to build your aerobic base, and about 20% in high-intensity zones
      (Zones 4 and 5) to challenge your cardiovascular ceiling.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

</CalculatorLayout>

